\section{Title of Invention}

Three-Layer Middle-Core Anatomically Biomimetic Sexual Aid Device

\section{Background of the Invention}

\subsection{Field of the Invention}

This invention relates to sexual aid devices, and more particularly to anatomically realistic devices featuring a novel three-layer construction that mimics natural biomechanical properties through innovative material layering.

\subsection{Description of Prior Art}

Current sexual aid devices typically employ one of two construction methods:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Dual-density construction}: A firm inner core with soft outer layer. This creates a rigid stick wrapped in soft material, failing to replicate natural flexibility.
\item \textbf{Uniform construction}: Single-material devices that are either entirely rigid or entirely flexible, lacking the complex biomechanical properties of natural anatomy.
\end{enumerate}

Neither approach successfully replicates the unique combination of:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item Surface compliance (soft outer tissue)
\item Structural rigidity (compression resistance for penetration)
\item Dynamic flexibility (ability to bend and conform to anatomical contours)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Summary of the Invention}

The present invention solves these deficiencies through a novel three-layer architecture:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Inner Core}: Highly flexible material (Shore A 5-10) that allows natural articulation
\item \textbf{Middle Structural Layer}: Semi-rigid material (Shore A 50-70) providing compression resistance and penetrative capability
\item \textbf{Outer Surface Layer}: Very soft material (Shore A 10-15) for realistic surface compliance
\end{enumerate}

This inverted approach to conventional dual-density design creates a flexural paradox where the device can bend dramatically at the core while maintaining penetrative rigidity through the middle structural layer, closely mimicking natural biomechanics.

